\startchapter[reference=sec:intro, title={Introduction}]

\startsection[title={How to Understand Cell Behavior}]
% Basic structure: Zoom in; What’s the big field, how to get from there to scRNA-Seq?

% big topic: cell biology and medicine
Every aspect of existence as thinking beings touches cell function.
Our body consists of cells performing the most varied tasks in order to maintain it:
they constitute organs, muscle, and everything else apart from teeth and bone, which are produced by cells.
We are protected by the complex interplay of the cells in our immune system, and the physical barrier of the dead keratinous cells making up our outer skin layer.
Even our thinking depends on neurons, our psyche on cellular regulation of our brain chemistry.
Formed by imperfect evolution, this machinery is not built to last.
Health, life and death are therefore topics every human has to confront at some point in life.

% complex diseases need better tools
Medicine has performed incredible feats of extending our expected span and quality of life.
In our consequently aging society, it becomes increasingly important to understand the long term effects of living.
The more diseases we can effectively counter, the more it is possible to shift our focus to rarer or more complex ones.
This includes diseases like cancer, caused by mutated cells, or diseases caused by malfunctioning cell regulation.
All those diseases have in common that they cannot be understood without knowing the cellular mechanisms involved.

\startplacefigure[reference=fig:dogma, location=left, title={The central dogma}]
\hbox{\input{imgs/dogma.tikz}}
\stopplacefigure

% motivate RNA-Seq
Each inner process of a cell is performed directly or indirectly through proteins reshaping themselves while binding to metabolic molecules and each other.
Their tasks range from simply transporting oxygen in red blood cells, over processing food into readily available energy, to complex developmental chain reactions forming our bodies in the womb.
Proteins achieve their versatility through the binding characteristics of their twenty different constituent amino acids.
Knowing what proteins are being produced in a cell at a given moment allows to draw conclusions about its purpose and health.
Proteomics, the measurement of proteins directly, is however made difficult by their complex binding behavior.
This results in proteomics being limited to targeted analysis of a small number of known proteins \cite[manzoni2018].
The assembly of proteins in cells is however fairly well understood and measurable.
The central dogma of molecular biology states that genes on our chromosomes are transcribed to short lived messenger RNA (mRNA) molecules, which are then translated to proteins (seed \infig[fig:dogma]).
%Genes are transcribed into a pre-messenger RNA (pre-mRNA) and then spliced into one of several possible final mRNAs encoding a different protein each.
The presence of mRNA in a cell therefore indicates that its respective protein is currently being produced.
It is possible to quantify and subsequently analyze snapshots of all mRNA molecules in a cell sample using RNA sequencing (RNA-Seq, \inlinecite[morin2008]).
This untargeted large-scale process is called gene expression analysis.
During the past decade, biological research worked with averaged RNA profiles of cell samples called bulk RNA-Seq.
Those profiles allow to capture the gene expression of a tissue or fluid sample without accounting for cell-to-cell variability.

\startplacefigure[reference=fig:cell-fate, title={
Cells differentiate smoothly and in stochasically diverging paths, from high potency (top) to high specialization (bottom).
Dedifferentiation (blue arrows) occurs naturally in plants and regenerating basal animals, but not humans.
The natural occurance of transdifferentiation (orange arrows) in humans has only been observed from α to β cells in pancreatic islets.
Experiments to artificially induce trans- or dedifferentiation have shown promise for regeneration of tissue damage and effects of aging.
\crlf\small{(Figure adapted from \inlinecite[gam2019] with permission)}
}]
%https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4409/8/10/1189
%\externalfigure[https://www.researchgate.net/publication/336222289/figure/fig1/AS:809726799523847@1570065321789/Cell-fate-plasticity-and-the-epigenetic-landscape-currently-applied-for-direct-cell.jpg][width=.8\textwidth]
\externalfigure[cell-fate.png][width=.8\textwidth]
\stopplacefigure

% SC revolution
Single cell RNA-Seq (scRNA-Seq) can measure extremely small expression values down to a single mRNA molecule \cite[tang2011].
This data comes with its own challenges, but enables the study of heterogeneous tissue, where expression averages from bulk RNA-Seq are not meaningful \cite[svensson2018b].
Nature Methods has named scRNA-Seq its method of the year 2013 together with scDNA-Seq \cite[nmeth2014].
The ability to dissect heterogeneity allows to improve our understanding of the complex cellular interaction, in developing, mature, and diseased tissue.
This enables new approaches to fight diseases like cancer \cite[patel2014,qian2017] and influencing cellular development (see \infig[fig:cell-fate]).
The second great advantage is that the amounts of data present in scRNA-Seq are necessary for machine learning approaches.
Advances in microfluidic devices have led to experimental protocols able to sequence more than a million cells \cite[macosko2015,klein2015,zheng2017].
The ample variation between this multitude of individual cells allows to derive patterns no human can detect by manual and careful isolation of bulk samples \cite[huang2018].
For example \inlinecite[plass2018] have determined the developmental lineage of every cell in a planarian, a regenerative species whose cellular mechanisms could be the key to prevent scarring or delay aging in humans.
\inlinecite[vento-tormo2018] have profiled cells from human placentas for regulatory interactions in the maternal-fetal interface, furthering our understanding of pregnancy diseases.
The international community of scientists working on transcriptomics is coming together to create the \n{Human Cell Atlas} \cite[regev2017] (see \infig[fig:hca]).

\startplacefigure[reference=fig:hca, title={
Human Cell Atlas (HCA) data flow overview.
\crlf\small{(Figure adapted from \inlinecite[hon2018] with permission)}
}]
\externalfigure[hca.jpg][width=.8\textwidth]
\stopplacefigure

%challenges: different sources of biases and noise
All these opportunities come at the cost of additional complexity and more confounding variables (biases).
For one, scRNA-Seq has by far fewer mRNA molecules per sample than bulk RNA-Seq, which results in noisier measurements in general \cite[brennecke2013].
Experimental protocols used for sequencing single cell transcriptomes have different tradeoffs between sensitivity, cost, time investment, and individual biases \cite[ziegenhain2017,vieth2019].
For example, microfluidic protocols cannot completely avoid lysing and sequencing two cells in the same droplet (doublets).
The number of cells that can be measured at once with a certain quality is therefore practically limited.
Measuring thousands or more cells without exploding costs means that shallow sequencing depths and additional biases are being tackled using computational solutions where applicable \cite[angerer2017].
% solutions
For example there are dedicated methods for doublet filtering \cite[mcginnis2019,wolock2019].
Not all effects however are correctly understood and approached.
So have studies suggested in past years that microfluidic protocols inflate the number of zeros in the data due to technical reasons, where a zero stands for a gene not being detected in a cell at all.
\inlinecite[svensson2020] however asserts that the high number of zeros stems from biological variation.
% preprocessing is important
Increased complexity in analysis can be conquered with improved modeling or more sophisticated preprocessing.
From-scratch modeling is more correct, but less flexible, as it requires careful informed design across the whole analysis process.
Each new analysis that significantly deviates from previous analyses requires a re-thinking and re-building of the model almost from the ground up.
There are however also modeling approaches that instead of performing analysis, replace several preprocessing steps specifically for scRNA-Seq data, like the normalizing single cell dimensionality reduction methods \n{scVI} \cite[lopez2018] and \n{GLM-PCA} \cite[townes2019].

% my work
The more practical approach is to start with careful preprocessing, transforming data in a way that fulfills the assumptions of subsequent analysis \cite[vieth2019].
The second step is then to create and use streamlined building blocks for pipelines to preprocess and analyze the data, and to then visualize the insights gained.
Until few years ago, there were no freely available programming environments that allowed this approach for single cell data analysis \cite[wolf2018,stuart2019].
Analysis pipelines came custom-built for a certain publication, just as hard to take apart and reuse as a holistic model would have been.
Every graduate student and postdoctoral researcher had to start from scratch.
The thesis at hand is a slice of the author’s continued efforts to change this.
My contributions to the field of scRNA-Seq data analysis include research and development of algorithms, data structures, pipelines, frameworks, and APIs.

\stopsection

In the following \insec[sec:workflow], I will provide an overview of a general scRNA-Seq analysis workflow,
specifically detailing steps I contributed to the most.
I worked on every computational step of the process except for alignment, with a specific focus on steps involving a count matrix.
Afterwards, \insec[sec:org] will address my contributions to the organization and reuse of analysis workflows like it.

\startsection[reference=sec:workflow, title={Workflow for scRNA-Seq Data Processing}]
% story: new challenges by single cell data; old way to deal with data (workflows) are not efficient → new approaches needed → me

% Overview:Describe how the workflows work and why the aforementioned is important.
The general workflow of scRNA-Seq data processing consists of five steps.
The first step, library preparation and sequencing, is experimental and is performed in a biological laboratory.
The second step, counting, creates a cell × gene matrix from raw data produced in step one.
The remaining steps are preprocessing, analysis and visualization of this matrix.

\startsubsection[title={Library Preparation and Sequencing}]
% 1. (cells → transcript library) and sequencing (transcript library → reads)
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:rna-seq, title={
ScRNA-Seq library preparation, sequencing, and mapping.
\bold{a)} In-vivo steps performed by the cell.
A gene is transcribed from DNA to a pre-messenger RNA (pre-mRNA).
Introns are spliced out to form one of several possible splicing variant mRNAs.
\bold{b)} In-vitro steps performed using lab equipment.
The mRNA strands are fragmented chemically and reverse transcribed into cDNA for stability, as RNA degrades quickly.
The fragments are tagged with a cell-specific barcode and optionally molecular and library identifiers.
The tagged fragments are pooled, amplified, then sequenced using a high-throughput sequencing machine.
\bold{c)} In-silico steps performed using a PC or server.
The tagged fragment sequences are aligned to a genome or transcriptome.
The mapped fragments are counted per cell-gene combination, accounting for splicing variants.
}]%
% https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f3/Summary_of_RNA-Seq.svg
\hbox{\input{imgs/rna-seq.tikz}}
\stopplacefigure

\noindent
The first step begins with an experiment to prepare and sequence a transcript library containing mostly mature mRNA but also unspliced mRNA and other RNA (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]a).
There are different kinds of library preparation protocols, all of which have in common that they isolate and fragment the RNA per single cell, reverse transcribe it into complementary DNA (cDNA) and then tag each fragment with a cell specific barcode (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]b).
Due to the low amount of RNA in single cells and the protocols’ detection limits, all current protocols also contain contain a step amplifying the available cDNA \cite[islam2014].
The most important differences between protocols manifest in cost, sensitivity and the distinction between those capturing paired fragments starting at both ends of the RNA and those capturing only the RNA end where translation into proteins starts, called the 3′ end \cite[ziegenhain2018].
The sequencing machine finally digitizes the fragments and allows all further processing to happen on a computer (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]c).
% 1½. protocol specific bias correction
Additional to the cell barcode, some protocols allow to enrich the library with information that can be used to reduce sources of noise or bias.
This includes e.\,g. unique molecular identifiers (UMIs), which allow to count each transcript only once, despite the amplification step \cite[islam2014].
A second example is adding known quantities of RNA from a foreign organism (spike-in RNA) to each cell library, which later allows to correct for nonlinear per-cell biases in transcript counts \cite[lun2017b].
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={Counting of transcripts or reads}]
% 2. (reads → counts)
Counting starts from the paired sequences read from both ends of a fragment (paired-end reads) or the 3′ end sequences (single-end reads).
The read or read pair includes a cell barcode and possibly a UMI barcode.
Classic counting uses specialized short read alignment tools like \n{Bowtie} \cite[langmead2009,langmead2012] or \n{STAR} \cite[dobin2013] to match reads to a reference genome annotated with information about where genes start, end, and can be spliced (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]c).
Aligned reads are counted per splicing configuration that translates to a protein, while collapsing the counts for reads with identical UMIs – if available (see \infig[fig:rna-seq]c).
Since only the resulting count matrix is needed for many kinds of downstream analysis, there have been advances in pseudo-alignment algorithms like \n{kallisto} \cite[ntranos2016] or \n{Salmon} \cite[patro2017], which efficiently count without a separate alignment step.
For ease of use, pipelines wrapping those tools for certain experimental protocols exist.
Those include \n{Cell Ranger} for the \n{10x Genomics} \n{Chromium} protocol \cite[zheng2017] or \n{zUMIs} \cite[parekh2018] for several protocols involving UMIs.

Protocols like the above produce counts for all expressed genes, resulting in a sparse matrix of not very sensitively captured expression values.
My contributions to the analysis process were among the first tools able to work with sparse matrices.
I also contributed to a new, targeted RNA-Seq protocol called BART-Seq \cite[uzbas2019] (see \insec[sec:bart-seq]).
Its tradeoffs are the inverse, allowing to sensitively capture expression values of a low number of target genes for a high number of samples.
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[reference=sec:preprocessing, title={Preprocessing of counts}]
% 3. (counts → expressions)
% generally \cite[luecken2018]: https://www.embopress.org/doi/10.15252/msb.20188746

To obtain comparable quantities, counts have to be preprocessed to correct for various technical biases.
Commonly one starts by eliminating rows and columns from the count matrix that are unlikely to be (living) cells or important genes.
This filtering removes rows and columns containing a low count sum or very few entries.
Rows matching these criteria are assumed to be empty droplets or wells, while matching columns are genes which due to their low counts do not impact later analysis steps.
It also removes cells containing a high fraction of mitochondrial gene counts, indicative of dead or dying cells \cite[ilicic2016].
Doublets cannot be detected reliably by a simple count threshold, as a pair of small cells can have similar numbers of expressed genes and counts as single large cell, but more sophisticated solutions are emerging \cite[mcginnis2019,wolock2019].
\inlinecite[luecken2019] point out that each of those filtering covariates in isolation is not sufficient to identify a non-cell row, and suggests conservative joint scoring using more than one covariate.

\startplacefigure[reference=fig:batch-effect, title={
Batch effect correction reduces biases between data gathered in multiple sequencer runs.
Cells from well-normalized data will have similar expression profiles to cells from the other sequencer runs.\crlf
\small{(Figure reproduced with friendly permission from \inlinecite[buttner2019])}
}]
\externalfigure[batch-effect-correction.svg][width=.8\textwidth]
\stopplacefigure

Another common preprocessing step is normalization.
It entails correction for common technical sources of cell variation, as well as biological sources with a strong effect, such as the cell cycle:
when not studying the cell cycle itself, its variation can obscure the processes under investigation \cite[buettner2015].
If multiple runs of the sequencer were used to obtain the full data, there will be biases per run that can be reduced by using a dedicated batch effect correction algorithm (see \infig[fig:batch-effect]).
While older bulk correction effects such as \n{ComBat} \cite[johnson2007] have proven to be effective in the last years, a growing number of dedicated scRNA-Seq batch effect correction tools is becoming available \cite[haghverdi2018,lotfollahi2018,korsunsky2019,polanski2020].
Preprocessing can also involve scaling count values using a per-cell function derived from the aforementioned spike-ins.
All transformation have to be carefully balanced with their potential to over-correct and remove relevant biological variation.

A final common preprocessing step highly variable gene selection.
It reduces data by discarding genes with low variability, as they contribute little to the data’s variability using differently rigorous algorithms \cite[brennecke2013,yip2018].
This more complex filtering is performed in addition to the aforementioned quality-based gene filtering, reducing the retained number of genes to the low thousands \cite[luecken2019].

One step lies between preprocessing and analysis.
In order to model and analyze count data, it is almost always necessary to calculate distances between cells’ expression profiles.
Distance measures usually expect data with no mean–variance dependency, i.\,e. normally distributed data, which can be fulfilled in two ways.
One is to transform expressions into a space where those expectations hold, commonly by logarithmizing it.
The intuition is that in in log-space, distances represent log-fold changes, an usual way to compare expression levels \cite[brennecke2013,eling2018].
Another way is to employ dimensional reduction methods to embed the data into a lower-dimensional space with the desired properties, such as Principal Component Analysis (PCA, \inlinecite[abdi2010]) or diffusion maps \cite[coifman2005].
An advantage of operating on a low-dimensional embedding instead of on the expression level are the reduced computational and memory costs.
However, \inlinecite[svensson2020] suggested that log-transforming data is a possible cause for distorting data by zero-inflation.
With single cell data specific transforms like \n{SCTransform} \cite[hafemeister2019], as well as embeddings like \n{scVI} \cite[lopez2018] or \n{GLM-PCA} \cite[townes2019], promising alternatives are emerging, despite them losing the log-fold interpretability.
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={Analysis of cellular dynamics, heterogeneity and gene roles}]
% 4. uncover dynamics, heterogeneity structure, variable importance and role
Analysts work interactively, with gradual insight gain shaping further steps.
To this end, overview statistics and visualizations are employed from the earliest to the last steps of an analysis (see \insec[sec:visualization]).

Analysis of single cell data generally focuses on uncovering previously unknown heterogeneity and its sources \cite[patel2014,poirion2016,angerer2017].
The kind of heterogeneity to uncover depends on the kind of data available:
the simplest case is discrimination between discrete subpopulations of few clearly different cell types.
A common goal there is to find a small number of marker genes able to reliably distinguish a subpopulation that is sufficiently distinct from the rest of the cells.
Methods performing this search are called differential gene expression (DE) analyses \cite[soneson2018].
While DE analysis methods stem from bulk RNA-Seq, single cell specific methods such as SCDE \cite[kharchenko2014], MAST \cite[finak2015], and BASiCS \cite[vallejos2016] outperform bulk methods due to the additional information in single cell data.

Another source of heterogeneity is the composition of the studied tissue.
Tumors as well as healthy tissue are composed of cells fulfilling a multitude of different roles that can only be uncovered using single cell analysis \cite[patel2014,zhang2016,qian2017].
A common method to make sense of complex heterogeneous data is cluster analysis.
For single cell data, the multi-resolution community detection algorithm \n{Leiden} \cite[traag2019] and its predecessor \n{Louvain} is used most commonly, despite \inlinecite[kiselev2019] arguing its lack of performance for smaller datasets.
A general problem of clustering is data reuse.
Clusters are commonly the basis for further analysis like inter-cluster DE analysis – a task the aforementioned single cell specific DE tools are specifically designed for.
However, this leads to both clusters and markers being derived from the same data, and treating clusters as ground truth in this setting leads to inflated confidence in the found markers.
The problem can be circumvented by joint clustering and DE analysis, manually propagating uncertainties \cite[zhang2019], or not relying on clustering to detect genes \cite[angerer2020].

\startplacefigure[reference=fig:heterogeneity-analysis, title={
    Heterogeneity Analysis approaches.
    For visualization purposes, cells are represented in a dimensionally reduced space represented by the outer border of \bold{a}, \bold{b}, and \bold{c}.
    All three represent common starting points for further analysis.
    \bold{a)} Clustering divides the set of cells into discrete subsets (clusters) based on similarity.
    The clarity of division between clusters shows how easily a given pair of clusters can be discriminated between.
    \bold{b)} Transition State Analysis dissects metastable states and their transitions.
    This can accurately represent cell development or more gradual cell identities than discrete types.
    \bold{c)} Scoring results in each cell having an individual score in one or more schemas, defining likelihood or strength of a certain property.
    \bold{d)} Differential Expression Analysis allows to identify genes defining a a given subdivision, marking them for further analysis and providing insight into cell identities.
    \bold{e)} Labelling of clusters assigns them a cell type label, based on marker genes and other external knowledge.
    \bold{f)} Trajectory Analysis groups and orders cells by similar transition state rather than absolute similarities and therefore allows to reason about continuous processes like cellular development or spatial gradients.
    \bold{g)} Gene Module Analysis integrates previous knowledge about gene roles to gain insights about the likeliness of each cell fulfilling a given role.
    \bold{h)} Gene Relevance Analysis relates the location of a cell in the embedding back to the genes driving that localization.
}]
\hbox{\input{imgs/heterogeneity-analysis.tikz}}
\stopplacefigure
% Alternative:
% a) cell level discretization (clustering), then cluster labelling (cell type) …
% b) … then DE
% c) gene level discretization (correlation modules) to be able to interpret gene results
% d) cell networks (e.g. PAGA) for e) pseudotime (less discretization) f) dim. red. g) kernels like Gene Relevance
\TODO[not using “cell identities” elsewhere, unify]
\TODO[c not immediately mentioned. Mention that prior info is used for labelling (marker genes) and gene modules]
\TODO[maybe also composition differences between datasets (mapping between cell neighborhoods)?]

While cluster analysis strongly benefits from the availability of scRNA-Seq data, the field of trajectory analysis can only exist because of it.
Traditionally, cells were thought of exhibiting a discrete cell type each.
For cells that are dramatically specialized for one main role, this idea still holds.
Most tissues however contain a multitude of cells fulfilling multiple roles each, without fitting into a discrete and homogeneous type.
%developmental
This includes cells in the process of differentiating from a malleable pluripotent state to a more or less specialized mature state \cite[haghverdi2015].
While marker genes for certain cell roles or developmental steps exist, this complex and gradual nature of most tissues requires sophisticated analysis and visualization of single cell data.
An approach that uncovers a developmental direction and hierarchy in developmental snapshot data is called trajectory analysis.
Early analysis tools like \n{Wanderlust} \cite[bendall2014], or DPT \cite[haghverdi2016] can be effectively applied to simple developmental data.
But while cells usually get increasingly specialized during their path of development, the reverse direction (dedifferentiation) can also be observed (see \infig[fig:cell-fate]).
Furthermore, developing cells do not necessarily follow all steps of differentiation in the same order or magnitude.
This results in cells temporarily or sometimes permanently stuck as intermediate types.
The field of trajectory analysis is therefore constantly evolving to enable the analysis of complex tissues up to whole animals \cite[plass2018] using tools like SPRING \cite[weinreb2018a] or PAGA \cite[wolf2019].

Analyses depend on the availability of informative data.
Since classic scRNA-Seq is limited to spliced mRNAs, additional data modalities are necessary to infer dynamics like directionality in trajectory inference \cite[efremova2020].
As an example, RNA velocity indicates the direction in which cells differentiate and therefore enhances trajectory inference with directionality and more accuracy.
It is reconstructed from the relation of spliced and unspliced mRNA \cite[lamanno2018].
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[reference=sec:visualization, title={Visualization as Analysis steps and result}]
% 5. (results often too complex to understand as numbers)
% talk about dim red here and the fact that it’s hard to reason about how the input variables relate back to output – make path for GR coming to the rescue
\startplacefigure[reference=fig:embeddings, title={
Four increasingly complex 2D embeddings of the same differentiating cells colored by age in days \cite[guo2010].
\bold{a)} Two carefully selected pluripotency markers are not able to separate the differentiation paths.
\bold{b)} A PCA is able to differentiate distinct clusters.
\bold{c)} Diffusion maps clearly show bifurcations and smooth transitions.
\bold{d)} UMAPs use machine learning to flatten multiple kinds of differences into a 2D plot, as in this case both bifurcation events present in the data.
}]
\externalfigure[dim-red.pdf]
%\input{imgs/dim-red.tikz}
\stopplacefigure

Analysis of complex heterogeneity patterns requires methods of matching complexity to visualize their results.
Complex developmental hierarchies and other relations in the data need to be faithfully represented.
This commonly involves laying out points representing the cells in a flat plane while trying to maintain relationships such as developmental closeness (see \infig[fig:embeddings]).
The challenge of finding a fitting low dimensional representation lies in the multiple scales biological processes take place in \cite[hasenauer2015] (see \infig[fig:hybrid]).
\TODO[maybe find better citation? this one just mentions multi-scale processes, not dim red]
Therefore, as opposed to of simple dimensional reduction methods used for for distance calculations (see \insec[sec:preprocessing]), the preferred methods for visualization take this multi-scale nature into account.
Complex transforms are irreversible, so their resulting coordinates of cells in the visualization (the embedding) cannot be directly traced back to the input genes.

\startplacefigure[reference=fig:hybrid, location=left, title={
A so called hybrid image exemplifies how the same data can encode information on multiple scales.
In this painting, the highly frequency visual features are interpreted as a woman with a mirror while the low frequency details are interpreted as a skull \cite[oliva2006].
(“All is Vanity” by Charles Allan Gilbert, 1892)
}]
\externalfigure[allisvanity.jpg][width=5.3cm]
\stopplacefigure

Without additional data providing a ground truth about subpopulation identity, or clearly separated clusters that are inherently assigned genes driving their embedding, the process of validating an embedding is not automatic, requiring creative reasoning and literature research.
A panel of marker genes per cluster is derived from the data and cross-referenced with literature to annotate known cell types in the data.
When discovering novel subpopulations, their adjacency to known ones can be used to make assumptions about their role and test them.
An upcoming way to simplify the process are cell transcriptome atlases \cite[regev2017,schaum2018] that can be used as references to automatically look up your cells and annotate them \cite[abdelaal2019].
\stopsubsection

% start own contributions
In recent years, I have contributed to a class of changes that scientists are adopting in their approach to scRNA-Seq analysis.
My contributions focused on improving the ability of scientists to apply the theoretical steps outlined above to real data in praxis.
To this end, I worked on implementing and extending useful algorithms in projects such as \n{destiny} and \n{scanpy}.

\startsubsection[title={destiny}]
% efficient and flexible diffusion maps for R
My chronologically first contribution \n{destiny} is a user-friendly, flexible and fast R tool for three closely related scRNA-Seq analysis steps \cite[angerer2016].
It was created to enable dimensional reduction of both large, sparse scRNA-Seq data and qPCR data which benefits from explicit handling of missing values.
The only previously existing tool was impractically slow and memory intensive when applied to scRNA-Seq data and did not support censored values present in qPCR data.
Beyond routines for efficient computation and visualization of diffusion maps (see \insec[sec:diffmaps]), \n{destiny} offers routines for diffusion pseudo time \cite[haghverdi2016], and gene relevance (\inlinecite[angerer2020], see \insec[sec:gene-relevance]).
Its efficiency involves reuse of intermediate results from the diffusion map creation to calculate diffusion pseudo time between cells as well as to project new data into an existing diffusion map.
\stopsubsection

\stopsection

Apart from implementing and extending algorithms for analysis and visualization, I especially focused on improving the interoperability between such steps.
Before addressing my work in this field, I would like to outline the relevant changes in the process of scRNA-Seq analysis in the following sections.

\startsection[reference=sec:org, title={Organizing Analysis Workflows}]

% (I am the one who can create usable interfaces and/for pipelines and fill in the gaps where “cobblestones” are missing)

In the scientific landscape of 2015 onwards, I identified tasks that scientists spent disproportionate time on rebuilding.
By improving how individual steps integrate with each other, we could increase how often and well we interface with and build on each others’ work.
I consequently focused my work on improving interoperability and modularity of existing tools, pioneering technologies and software development processes in a research environment, and developed some of the missing building blocks for the resulting toolkit.

To demonstrate the need for rigorous software solutions like mine, I also analyzed sizes and sequencing depth of 455 publicly available datasets and the impact of growing data sizes (see \infig[fig:dataset-sizes]) on the field \cite[angerer2017].
Since then, effects of increasing data availability are increasingly felt throughout the field, while the theoretical possibilities mentioned in \inlinecite[angerer2017] are becoming reality.
For example, cell atlases containing large amounts of multimodal data can now be used to infer labels for new data using transfer learning \cite[abdelaal2019].

In this section, I outline the ways in which programmatic frameworks improve reusability and therefore interoperability and reproducibility, specifically of scRNA-Seq analysis.
I address how the field changed its approach to analysis workflows, and my contributions in facilitating the changes.

\startplacefigure[reference=fig:dataset-sizes, title={
Single cell dataset sizes uploaded to Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO).
This updated version of a figure from \inlinecite[angerer2017] shows the sustained trend of increasing datasets and cell numbers per dataset.
}]
\externalfigure[imgs/dataset-sizes.pdf]
\stopplacefigure

\startsubsection[title={Analysis Pipelines: Status Quo}]
% pipelines used scripts and binaries to proceed between snapshots
The steps necessary to reproduce the preprocessing and analysis of a publication are bundled in an executable bundle of scripts called a pipeline.
Pipelines perform transformations on the data, collect statistics, and compile reports.
Depending on the amount of time and resources required to run the whole pipeline, it is desirable be able to only repeat part of the processing in case of an error.
This is mostly achieved by regularly storing snapshot files representing the processing state between two subsequent sets of steps.
Before my work in the area started, pipelines were mostly written in R or \n{GNU make} and often executed a command line for each step, a procedure which by necessity consumed and produced a snapshot, but allows no choice when this high granularity is undesirable.
Despite the field’s increasing investment into machine learning algorithms implemented in Python, few tools were available in that language.

% communication
Communication with other languages or between pipeline steps involved reading an (often textual) snapshot from a file system, processing it, and writing it back to disk.
This process involved long run times due to slow reading and writing, high storage space requirements and little resilience to errors.
File corruption introduced e.\,g. via unreliable access to network storage could not be detected in text files, which lack checksums and other resilience precautions.
In practice, the lack of frameworks meant that each tool or pipeline had to solve common problems by itself, such as hardening against the aforementioned reliability issues.
This further wasted resources and increased the potential for errors.
The resulting pipeline code published with papers was very application-specific and hard to generalize or share.

% Existing methods are not focused on interoperability.
Even within analysis pipelines operating entirely in memory instead of on slow and unreliable files, individual tools were not necessarily designed to be compatible.
Single tools could halt progress if they had insufficient performance for upcoming large datasets from microfluidic protocols.
The tools’ varying code quality, choice of default values and error reporting led to subtle incompatibility bugs.
Furthermore, no data structures and file exchange formats suitable for scRNA-Seq existed, so workflows manually kept count matrices and annotation data frames in sync across filtering steps, increasing the potential for error.
Furthermore, reproducibility was hard to guarantee in the presence of nondeterministic methods and a multitude of tool versions with subtly different defaults and behavior.

% R: Bioconductor
In the R world, the \n{Bioconductor} project \cite[huber2015] provided tools for preprocessing and basic analysis, but little in the way of embeddings.
\n{Destiny} is well integrated into this ecosystem and, as mentioned earlier, closes functional gaps there.

% - pre-GR: differential expression
No dimensionality reduction method except for the simplistic linear PCA provides a way to relate embedding location back to input variables.
The popularity of nonlinear dimensionality reductions meant that there was a gap between using them as cell-level analysis tool and the differential expression (DE) analysis \cite[soneson2018] as most popular gene-level analysis tool.
DE requires discrete partitioning of the data, which is not well defined for data containing gradual transition of gene expression profiles between cells, and has the aforementioned issues with uncertainty propagation.
\stopsubsection

\startsubsection[title={Frameworks for modular scRNA-Seq Analysis Pipelines}]
% - What came before frameworks?
The original way to implement the aforementioned pipelines arose from the lowest common denominators of program interaction, summarized by Doug McIlroy as the UNIX philosophy \cite[salus1994]:

\setupdelimitedtext[blockquote][style=\slx, before={\setupinterlinespace[line=2.4ex]\noindentation}]
\startblockquote
Write programs that do one thing and do it well.\crlf
Write programs to work together.\crlf
Write programs to handle text streams, because that is a universal interface.
\stopblockquote

\noindentation
Text based file formats and pipeline steps realized as command line interfaces working on these files had their place for a long time.
The approach results in automatic availability of intermediate steps as snapshots, in a human readable form, and to use basic text manipulation to work on them.
Attempting to serve this lowest common denominator however lead to the aforementioned limitations when building pipelines. 

% - What is a framework?
The alternative is to use a framework, i.\,e. an opinionated set of data structures and routines that are tightly integrated but generic enough to be used as building blocks for pipelines or visualization tools.
This tight integration allows to rely on information being tracked that allows the framework to provide necessary features, which would have to be rebuilt on a case-by-case basis without a framework.
It also allows to bundle steps efficiently in memory, allowing control over when to invest the resources to store snapshots.

% - Through frameworks, data interchange formats and containers we get interoperability and reproducibility.
Due to their tighter coupling of data structures and algorithms, frameworks are specific to individual programming languages.
For scRNA-Seq analysis, the major frameworks are implemented in R and Python: \n{Seurat}\footnote{
Note that \n{Seurat} in its original publication 2015 was no general purpose scRNA-Seq analysis framework yet, but a data alignment algorithm with additional helper routines.
} \cite[satija2015,butler2018], \n{scater} \cite[mccarthy2017], and \n{scanpy} \cite[wolf2018] are the most popular ones.
Their data structures allow keeping gene and cell annotations and graphs in sync with the expression matrix while filtering and concatenating data (see \insec[sec:anndata]).
Interspersing steps from frameworks written in different languages stay possible via common serialization formats and in-memory conversion, while largely maintaining aforementioned advantages \cite[angerer2019b].

%reproducibility
Reproducibility seems to be largely solved, as widely available container solutions such as \n{Docker} allow defining perfectly reproducible environments \cite[ditommaso2015].
Unlike standalone tools, the frameworks also consistently support random seeding for identical output of nondeterministic processes.
% - good error reporting / software quality important for good science (Avoid garbage-in garbage-out)
% - principle of many eyes: less bugs in shared code
The number of users of a framework or tool contributes to its quality and therefore the quality of studies relying on it, as more people report errors and special use cases, which increases the robustness of the frameworks towards those problems \cite[kim2015b].
Bigger frameworks encompassing many tools have an easier time to test several permutations of their functionality than each tool would be able to when standing alone.

%R
The R world is more fragmented than Python’s:
The \n{Bioconductor} ecosystem provides annotated matrix data structures and tools for preprocessing scRNA-Seq data.
\n{Scater} provides extensive quality control and preprocessing but little for analysis \cite[luecken2019].
\n{Seurat} has opinionated preprocessing, analysis and plotting routines but is not integrated into \n{Bioconductor}.
Its data structure is \n{Seurat}-specific and needs conversion to the more generic \n{Bioconductor} data structures.
Standalone tools in R like \n{destiny} \cite[angerer2016], \n{scmap} \cite[kiselev2018], BASiCS \cite[eling2018], or \n{velocyto} \cite[lamanno2018] usually support \n{Bioconductor} data structures or even just plain matrices and data frames.

%AnnData and scanpy
In Python, many standalone tools start supporting the generic data structure \n{AnnData} \cite[zappia2018,eraslan2019,polanski2020].
\n{AnnData} supports reading and writing from and to several language agnostic interchange formats.
It was the first annotated matrix data structure to support sparse expression matrices and therefore up to millions of cells.
It provides its own format that supports disk backing for operations on data too large to be performed in memory, sparse or dense.
% Scanpy
\n{AnnData} was developed alongside \n{scanpy} in order to make the latter a highly modular framework.
\n{Scanpy} is now in widespread international use, continuously expanded with new algorithms, and on its way to become a community governed project \cite[wolf2018,zappia2018].
Being based on \n{NumPy} \cite[vanderwalt2011], \n{SciPy} \cite[virtanen2020], and other mature and highly performant Python technologies, \n{scanpy} is very scalable.
% - serving all MOs: iterative with biologists, manual coding, let it be done as-a-service
Integrating well with \n{JupyterLab} and \n{Jupyter} notebooks \cite[kluyver2016], \n{scanpy} lends itself to be used interactively to quickly iterate on analyses with close feedback from biologists.
It also supports being used as a Python library to back visualization tools, web servers and traditional file-based processing pipelines.
\stopsubsection

\stopsection

\stopchapter

\stopcomponent
